{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["HaShem <b>בָּדָד יַנְחֶנּוּ</b> ('alone did He guide him'). It may be explained according to the statement of the Sages of blessed memory (Tanhuma, Tzav 7) on the verse (Hosea 4:17): ״חֲבוּר עֲצַבִּים אֶפְרַיִם הַנַּח לוֹ״ — when there is unity among Israel, even if they are idolaters, הַנַּח לוֹ ('leave him alone'), meaning in the sense of (Esther 2:11) ״וַהֲנָחָה לַמְּדִינוֹת עָשָׂה״. And this is the meaning of His saying ״ה׳ בָּדָד״ — meaning, when the people are בָּדָד, in the sense of בַּד (a single unit/united), HaShem guides them." "<b>וְאֵין עִמּוֹ אֵל נֵכָר</b> ('and no foreign god with him') — meaning, even though they are idolaters, HaShem does not reckon this sin against them. And the statement ״אֵין עִמּוֹ״ may be interpreted according to the way the Sages of blessed memory expounded (Sukkah 41a) on the verse (Jeremiah 30:17): ״דּוֹרֵשׁ אֵין לָהּ״ — by inference, there is [someone who seeks her]; so too in our case He says ״אֵין עִמּוֹ״ — by inference, there is with him — except that HaShem does not punish him for this." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend along this line: When is it that ״ה׳ בָּדָד יַנְחֶנּוּ״? When the evil portion has been eliminated from Israel. And that is what it says: ״וְאֵין עִמּוֹ אֵל נֵכָר״ — which is the evil portion attached to a person through a person's sin. And the discerning will understand:"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:21:24.521578888Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}